That makes total sense. Focusing on Vanilla JavaScript (HTML/CSS/JS) is the best way to master the fundamentals of the "Document Object Model" (DOM) and logic before adding complex backends.
Here are 5 high-impact projects you can build using only frontend code:
## 1. Interactive Expense Tracker (Local Storage)
This proves you can handle data without a database.

* The Project: A dashboard where users enter expenses (name, amount, category). It calculates total balance, income, and expenses.
* The "Pro" Feature: Use localStorage so that when the user refreshes the page, their data is still there.
* JS Skills: JSON.stringify(), filter(), reduce(), and DOM manipulation.

## 2. Custom Video Player
Don’t just use the default browser controls; build your own UI.

* The Project: A clean video interface with custom Play/Pause buttons, a progress bar that you can click to "scrub" through the video, volume control, and playback speed toggles.
* JS Skills: Working with the HTML5 Media API and addEventListener for time updates.

## 3. Movie Discovery App (API Integration)
This is the best way to show you can work with external data.

* The Project: Use the free TMDB API to fetch movie data. Create a search bar that shows movie posters, ratings, and descriptions in a grid layout.
* JS Skills: fetch(), async/await, and dynamically creating HTML elements based on API results.

## 4. Kanban "Drag & Drop" Board
This is a classic productivity tool (like Trello).

* The Project: Three columns (To Do, Doing, Done). Users can create "cards" and physically drag them from one column to another.
* The "Pro" Feature: Add a "Dark Mode" toggle using CSS variables and JS.
* JS Skills: The Drag and Drop API (dragstart, dragover, drop).

## 5. Multi-Step Quiz App with Timer
A great test of state management.

* The Project: A quiz that shows one question at a time. It tracks the score, has a countdown timer for each question, and shows a "Results" screen at the end with a progress circle.
* JS Skills: setInterval(), arrays of objects, and conditional rendering (showing/hiding sections).

